> Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.

Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\| Sure, here's a simple C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text. This function uses a basic conversion for bold and italic text, as well as headers. More complex markdown features can be added as needed.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <map>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::map<std::string, std::string> replacements = {
        {"**", "<strong>"},
        {"*", "<em>"},
        {"#", "##"},
        {"##", "<h2>"},
        {"###", "<h3>"},
        {"####", "<h4>"},
        {"#####", "<h5>"},
        {"######", "<h6>"}
    };

    std::istringstream stream(markdown);
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(stream, line)) {
        for (const auto& pair : replacements) {
            size_t pos = 0;
            while ((pos = line.find(pair.first, pos)) != std::string::npos) {
                line.replace(pos, pair.first.length(), pair.second);
                pos += pair.second.length();
            }
        }
        html += line + "\n";
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function uses a simple string replacement to convert markdown to HTML. It currently supports bold (`**text**`), italic (`*text*`), and headers (`# Header`, `## Header`, etc.). More complex markdown features can be added as needed. Note that this function does not handle nested markdown or other markdown features like links, images, or lists.

